Raw example: {'messages': [{'content': 'You will be given a competitive programming problem. Please reason step by step about the solution, then provide a complete implementation in C++17.\n\nYour solution must read input from standard input (cin), write output to standard output (cout).\nDo not include any debug prints or additional output.\n\nPut your final solution within a single code block:\n```cpp\n<your code here>\n```\n\n# Problem\n\nGiven a string s, find the number of ways to split s to substrings such that if there are k substrings (p1, p2, p3, ..., pk) in partition, then pi = pk - i + 1 for all i (1 ≤ i ≤ k) and k is even.\n\nSince the number of ways can be large, print it modulo 109 + 7.\n\nExecution time limit: 3.0 seconds\nMemory limit: 256.0 MB\n\n## Input Format\nThe only line of input contains a string s (2 ≤ |s| ≤ 106) of even length consisting of lowercase Latin letters.\n\n## Output Format\nPrint one integer, the number of ways of partitioning the string modulo 109 + 7.\n\n## Examples\n```input\nabcdcdab\n```\n```output\n1\n```\n-----\n```input\nabbababababbab\n```\n```output\n3\n```\n\n## Note\nIn the first case, the only way to partition the string is ab|cd|cd|ab.\n\nIn the second case, the string can be partitioned as ab|b|ab|ab|ab|ab|b|ab or ab|b|abab|abab|b|ab or abbab|ab|ab|abbab.\n', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': '<think>\nOkay, I need to solve this problem where I have to find the number of ways to split a string into substrings such that the partition is symmetric and has an even number of parts. Let\'s think about how to approach this.\n\nSo, the key conditions are:\n1. The partition must have an even number of parts (k is even).\n2. For each i, pi must equal pk-i+1. So the first part equals the last, the second equals the second last, etc.\n\nHmm. So the entire string must be partitioned into pairs of equal substrings, arranged symmetrically. For example, if there are 4 parts, then part1 == part4 and part2 == part3.\n\nWait, but the number of partitions is even, but the order is such that each pair mirrors each other. So the structure is like p1 p2 ... p(k/2) p(k/2+1) ... pk, but p(k/2+1) is p(k/2) reversed in the pair? Wait, no. Because for i from 1 to k, pi must equal pk-i+1. So for k even, the first half is mirrored in the second half. So for k=4, p1=p4 and p2=p3.\n\nSo the partition must be a palindrome in terms of the sequence of substrings. The entire string is split into 2m parts where m is a positive integer, and the first m parts are the reverse of the last m parts.\n\nBut how do we model this? The problem is to count all possible such partitions.\n\nLet me think of dynamic programming. Maybe we can track the possible positions where a partition can be made such that the current part\'s mirror is already present.\n\nAlternatively, maybe precompute all possible prefixes that can form a valid first part, and then recursively check the remaining string.\n\nBut given the constraints on the input size (up to 1e6), any O(n^2) approach would be too slow. So we need an O(n) or O(n log n) solution.\n\nAnother idea: For the entire string to be split into an even number of parts with the mirror condition, the entire string must be a palindrome. Because each pair (p_i, p_{k-i+1}) must be equal, and concatenated in order. Wait, no. Wait, the entire string would be p1 p2 ... p_{m} p_{m} ... p2 p1, where m is k/2. So the entire string is a concatenation of p1 p2 ... pm followed by pm ... p2 p1. So the entire string would not necessarily be a palindrome. For example, in the first sample input: ab|cd|cd|ab. The entire string is abcdcdab. The parts are ab, cd, cd, ab. The first two parts are ab and cd, then their mirrors cd and ab. So the entire string is ab cd cd ab. The entire string is a palindrome when considering the parts as a sequence, but the concatenated string is not a palindrome. Wait, no: abcdcdab as a whole is not a palindrome. So the entire string doesn\'t have to be a palindrome. So that idea is out.\n\nSo the approach must allow for splitting into mirrored pairs of substrings, but the entire string isn\'t necessarily a palindrome.\n\nHmm. So how do we model the possible splits?\n\nLet me think recursively. Suppose we have a split into parts p_1, p_2, ..., p_2m. Then p_1 must equal p_2m, p_2 equals p_2m-1, etc. So for the split to be valid, the entire string must be split into even number of parts, and each part has a mirror in the opposite position.\n\nThis suggests that the split can be built by finding a first part p1, then the last part must be equal to p1, and then the remaining inner substring (from after p1 to before the last part) must form a valid split with 2(m-1) parts. But then this inner substring must also split into pairs.\n\nWait, but the inner substring could be split into 2(m-1) parts, but the order of the parts in the inner substring would need to mirror each other. So perhaps the problem can be approached using dynamic programming, where dp[i] represents the number of valid splits for the substring s[0..i).\n\nBut how to compute dp[i]?\n\nAnother approach: For each position i, the number of ways to split the substring up to i into a valid partition. Let\'s see. The entire string must be partitioned into an even number of parts, with the mirror condition.\n\nLet me think of the split as follows: Each split is a sequence of parts that can be grouped into pairs. The first part and the last part must be equal. The second part and the second last must be equal, etc. So the entire split must consist of pairs of equal substrings arranged symmetrically.\n\nSo the split can be built by choosing pairs from the left and right. For example, the first step is to choose a prefix p1 and a suffix p2k, which must be equal. Then the remaining substring (after p1 and before p2k) must be split into 2(k-1) parts, following the same rule.\n\nThis suggests that the problem can be solved using a two-pointer approach, possibly with some hashing or prefix/suffix matching.\n\nBut considering that the string can be up to 1e6 characters, we need an efficient way to compare possible prefix and suffix pairs quickly.\n\nHashing could be useful here. Precompute prefix hashes and suffix hashes, so that for any possible split point, we can compare the prefix and suffix in O(1) time.\n\nBut how do we model the possible splits? Let\'s think of the split as follows:\n\nThe entire string is split into 2m parts. For each possible m, the parts must form a palindrome in pairs. So for m=1, there are two parts which must be equal. For m=2, four parts: p1 p2 p3 p4, where p1=p4 and p2=p3.\n\nBut how do we count all possible such splits?\n\nAlternative approach: The split can be built recursively. The first part must be a prefix of the string, and the last part must be the same as the first part. Then, the remaining part (the substring between the first and last part) must form a valid split in the same way.\n\nSo, for example, the split is:\n\ns = p1 + s1 + p1\'\n\nwhere p1\' is equal to p1, and s1 must form a valid split with an even number of parts (which could be zero, but since the total number of parts must be even, s1 would have an even number of parts minus 2, which would be even again). Wait, but the total number of parts is 2 + (number of parts in s1). Since the total must be even, the number of parts in s1 must also be even.\n\nWait, no. The total number of parts is 2m. So if the first and last parts are each of length l, then the remaining substring s1 has to be split into 2(m-1) parts. So s1 must be partitioned in the same way, but with two fewer parts.\n\nSo the problem can be modeled using dynamic programming. Let dp[i][j] be the number of valid ways to split the substring s[i..j) (i inclusive, j exclusive) into an even number of parts with the required symmetry. Then, the base case is when the substring is empty, which has 1 way (zero parts). For non-empty substrings, we can iterate over possible splits where the first k characters form a prefix, and the last k characters form a suffix equal to the prefix, then the inner part (i +k to j -k) is processed recursively.\n\nBut this approach is O(n^3), which is way too slow for n=1e6. So we need a smarter way.\n\nAlternative idea: The problem is similar to the problem of counting the number of ways to split a string into palindromic substrings, but here the condition is different. However, perhaps the solution can be found using a greedy approach with some precomputed hash values.\n\nWait, but how?\n\nLet me consider the first sample input: "abcdcdab". The valid split is ab|cd|cd|ab. Here, the first two parts are ab and cd, and the last two are cd and ab, which are the reverses. So the entire split has 4 parts. So the way to split is to take the first two parts, then their reverses.\n\nAlternatively, the split can be built by finding a chain of pairs. For example, the split is formed by taking a prefix p1, then after p1, another prefix p2, then the remaining part of the string must end with p2 and then p1. So perhaps the split can be represented as p1 + p2 + ... + p2 + p1.\n\nBut the number of parts must be even, so the split is made up of pairs. So, the split is built by a series of such pairs.\n\nAnother approach: Let\'s model the problem as building a stack of prefixes. For each possible prefix, when we find a matching suffix, we can push the position and then recursively process the remaining substring. The number of ways is the product of the possibilities at each step.\n\nBut how to manage this efficiently?\n\nAlternatively, the problem can be viewed as a series of layers. Each layer contributes a pair of equal parts. For example, the first layer is the outermost pair (p1 and p2k), the next layer is the pair (p2 and p2k-1), etc. Each layer must be split into pairs, and the total number of layers must be even.\n\nWait, perhaps the total number of layers (k/2) can vary, but k must be even.\n\nBut how do we model the layers? Let\'s think of the process as follows: start with the entire string. For each possible split into p1 and the remaining part (which must end with p1), then the remaining part (after p1 and before the final p1) is processed again in the same way.\n\nEach time we split off a p from the beginning and the end, we add 2 to the count of parts, and the problem reduces to the inner substring.\n\nThe base case is when the inner substring is empty, which gives a valid split. But the total number of parts would be 2m, where m is the number of layers.\n\nBut how to count all possible such splits? Each time you split off a prefix and a suffix (equal to the prefix), the inner part must be split in the same way.\n\nSo the number of ways for the entire string is equal to the sum over all possible splits into p1 and p1, multiplied by the number of ways to split the inner substring.\n\nWait, but this seems recursive. For example, let\'s say the entire string starts with a prefix p and ends with p. Then the inner substring (after p and before the ending p) must be split into even parts in the same way.\n\nSo the total number of ways for the entire string is the sum over all possible p (prefixes of the string) that are also suffixes of the string, of the number of ways to split the inner substring. Plus, the case where the entire string is split into two parts (p and p), which is a base case.\n\nWait, but the inner substring can have splits, which may add more pairs. So the recursion is:\n\nf(s) = sum over all possible p (prefix of s) where s starts with p and ends with p, of (f(inner) + 1), where the +1 is the case where the inner is empty. Wait, no. Because the +1 would correspond to the case where the inner is split into zero parts, which is allowed (since 0 is even). So for each split into p and p, the inner can be split in any valid way (including zero parts), which would contribute f(inner) + 1 (if f(inner) is defined as the number of ways for the inner substring). Wait, maybe the base case is that an empty string has 1 way (zero parts). Then, for a non-empty string, the number of ways is the sum over all possible p (where s starts with p and ends with p) of the number of ways for the inner substring. Because each such p allows the split into p and p, and the inner can be split in f(inner) ways, which may add more pairs.\n\nSo for example, if the entire string is \'abababab\', then possible splits are:\n\n- split into \'ab\' and \'ab\', with inner \'abab\'. The inner can be split into \'ab\' and \'ab\', inner empty. So this gives a way with two pairs (total 4 parts). Or the inner can be split into \'a\' and \'a\', and \'b\' and \'b\', but that would require that the inner \'abab\' starts and ends with \'a\', but \'abab\' starts with \'a\' and ends with \'b\', which is not possible. So perhaps for the inner \'abab\', the possible split is into \'ab\' and \'ab\', leading to two ways. Wait, but in this example, the entire split would be \'ab|ab|ab|ab\', which is a valid split. So the total ways would be 1 (the split into two parts) plus the possibilities of the inner.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m getting confused here. Let\'s re-express the recursive function.\n\nLet f(s) be the number of valid splits for the string s. Then:\n\nf(s) = sum over all possible prefixes p of s such that s starts with p and ends with p, and p is not the entire s (since the split must have even parts, so the entire split into two parts is allowed if possible), of (f(inner) + 1), where inner is the substring between p and p.\n\nWait, but for the split into two parts, the inner is empty, so f(inner) is 1 (the empty string can be split into zero parts). So the +1 might not be necessary. Wait, perhaps f(s) is the sum of all possible ways of splitting the first and last part as p, and then adding the number of ways for the inner.\n\nFor example, if the entire string is p + s\' + p, then the number of ways is sum over all possible p (including the entire string, but in that case s\' is empty) of (f(s\')).\n\nBut since the split must have an even number of parts, splitting into two parts (p and p) is allowed. So the entire split would be two parts (even), so that\'s a valid split. So for the case where s\' is empty, f(s\') is 1 (the base case). So for any possible p where s starts and ends with p, the total ways for the split is 1 (the split into two parts) plus the ways to split s\' into even parts. Wait, no. Because the split into two parts counts as one way. Then, for each split into p and p, and the inner s\' can be split into even parts. So the total ways for that particular p is 1 (split into two parts) multiplied by the number of ways to split s\' into even parts. Wait, perhaps not. Because the split into two parts (p and p) is a single way, and if the inner s\' can be split into k ways, then each of those k ways would correspond to adding more pairs. So for example, if the inner s\' can be split into m ways, then the total ways for this p is 1 + m. Because you can split into two parts (adding 1 way), or split into two parts plus the splits of the inner s\' (adding m ways). But no, that\'s not correct. Because when you split into p and p, and then split the inner s\' into even parts, that forms a split of the entire string into 2 + (number of parts in s\') parts. But the problem requires the entire split to have even parts. Since the inner s\' is split into even parts (say 2m parts), then the total parts would be 2 + 2m = even, which is allowed. So each split of the inner s\' into even parts contributes a way. So for each possible p, the number of ways contributed is the number of ways to split s\' into even parts (including zero ways if s\' can\'t be split). So the total for p is 1 (the split into two parts) plus the ways for s\'. Wait, no. If s\' can be split into m ways, then each of those m ways is a split into even parts. So for each such split, the total split would be p followed by the splits of s\' followed by p. But the total parts would be 2 + (number of parts in s\' split). But the split into two parts (p and p) is allowed (s\' is empty, which has 1 way). So the split into p and p is one way. If s\' can be split into m ways, each of those would add m more ways for the entire string. So the total for p is 1 + f(s\') ?\n\nWait, let\'s see an example. Suppose s is \'abababab\'. Suppose p is \'ab\'. Then s\' is \'abab\'. The split into two parts is \'ab\' + \'ab\' (1 way). Then s\' is \'abab\', which can be split into \'ab\' and \'ab\' (1 way). So the total for p \'ab\' would be 1 (split into two parts) plus 1 (split into four parts: \'ab\' + \'ab\' + \'ab\' + \'ab\'). So the total is 2. But according to the problem\'s first sample, the split is into four parts. So how does this fit into the model?\n\nWait, in the first sample, the input is \'abcdcdab\', which is split into four parts. So the split is p1=ab, p2=cd, p3=cd, p4=ab. So for the entire string, the first split is into p1 and p4 (ab and ab), and the inner is \'cdcd\', which is split into p2 and p3 (cd and cd). So in this case, the total ways for the entire string is 1 (split into two parts) multiplied by 1 (split of the inner into two parts). So the total ways is 1 * 1 = 1. But according to the recursive idea, the sum for p would be the number of ways to split the inner. Wait, perhaps the recursive formula is multiplicative. Because when you split into p and p, the inner s\' can be split in f(s\') ways, each of which contributes a split of the entire string into 2 + 2m parts. So the total number of ways for p is f(s\'). Including the case where s\' is split into zero parts (i.e., m=0), which is the split into two parts. Wait, maybe the base case is that the empty string has f(empty) = 1. So when you split into p and p, the inner s\' is empty, that gives 1 way. If the inner s\' is non-empty, then f(s\') gives the number of ways to split s\', and each of those ways corresponds to adding more pairs. So the total for p is f(s\'). So for example, when the inner is empty, f(s\') is 1, which counts the split into two parts. When the inner s\' can be split into m ways, each way adds m more splits.\n\nSo the recursive formula would be:\n\nf(s) = sum over all possible p where s starts with p and ends with p of f(s\').\n\nWhere s\' is the substring between p and p.\n\nThe base case is f(empty) = 1.\n\nBut how do we compute this efficiently?\n\nFor example, in the first sample input: abcdcdab. Let\'s see possible p\'s:\n\n- p could be \'a\', but the string ends with \'b\', so no.\n\nWait, no. The entire string starts with \'a\' and ends with \'b\', so the first and last characters are different. So no possible p of length 1.\n\nNext possible p of length 2: \'ab\'. The string starts with \'ab\' and ends with \'ab\', so yes. The inner s\' is \'cdcd\'. So we compute f(\'cdcd\').\n\nFor \'cdcd\', possible p\'s are \'c\' (no, ends with \'d\'), \'cd\' (starts with \'cd\' and ends with \'cd\'). So inner is empty. So f(\'cdcd\') = f(empty) = 1. So the total for the original string is sum over p=ab of f(\'cdcd\') = 1. Which matches the first sample.\n\nAnother example: the second sample input, \'abbababababbab\', which has output 3.\n\nSo for each possible split into p and p, then the inner s\' is processed. The sum over all possible p of f(s\') gives the total ways.\n\nBut the problem is that for a string of length up to 1e6, this approach would take O(n^2) time, which is impossible.\n\nSo we need a way to compute f(s) efficiently.\n\nAlternative idea: The function f(s) can be computed using dynamic programming with memoization, but for large n, this is not feasible. So perhaps there\'s a way to model this using a linear approach.\n\nLet me think of the problem in terms of the positions where the splits can be made.\n\nEach valid split must be a sequence of pairs of equal substrings. The first split is at some position k, such that the first k characters are p, and the last k characters are p. Then, the remaining string (from k to len(s) -k) must also be split into pairs.\n\nSo for the entire string, the number of ways is the sum over all possible k (where the first k and last k characters are equal) of the number of ways to split the middle part.\n\nThis suggests that the problem can be solved using a dynamic programming approach where dp[i][j] represents the number of ways to split the substring s[i..j) into valid even partitions.\n\nBut for n up to 1e6, even O(n^2) is impossible. So this approach is not feasible.\n\nAlternative idea: Note that each split must be done in pairs. So the entire process is a series of steps where each step splits off a pair of equal substrings from the start and end.\n\nSo, the total number of ways is equal to the product of the number of choices at each step where such a split is possible.\n\nFor example, imagine that the string can be split into pairs at multiple levels. Each step, we can choose a possible split (p and p), then proceed to the inner string. The total ways are the product of the number of possible splits at each step.\n\nSo, the number of ways is the product of the number of possible splits at each layer.\n\nBut how do we model this?\n\nLet\'s think recursively. The number of ways for the current string is the sum of the number of possible splits for each possible first split. For each possible split into p and p, the number of ways contributed is the number of ways to split the inner substring.\n\nBut how to compute this for large strings?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem can be solved using a greedy approach. We can compute the maximum possible number of layers, and for each layer, find how many possible splits there are. Then the total ways would be the product of the number of choices at each layer.\n\nBut how?\n\nAnother idea: For the entire string, the minimal possible split is two parts. But there can be multiple splits. For example, in the second sample, the splits can have different numbers of layers. So, the layers can vary, and each possible path through the layers contributes a way.\n\nBut again, for large n, this approach is not feasible.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem can be modeled using memoization and hashing. For each possible substring, precompute the possible split points and memoize the result. But for 1e6, the number of substrings is O(n^2), which is impossible.\n\nAlternative approach: Let\'s consider the problem from the beginning of the string. We can track possible positions where a split can be made, and accumulate the number of ways up to that position.\n\nWait, let\'s consider dynamic programming where dp[i] is the number of ways to split the first i characters into valid splits with even number of parts. Then, for each i, we can compute dp[i] based on previous dp values.\n\nBut how to model the even number of parts and the mirror condition.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the problem using a stack-based approach, where each time we find a prefix that matches a suffix, and multiply the possibilities.\n\nBut I\'m stuck.\n\nLet me think about the structure of the split. The split must form a palindrome of even parts. So, the split is a sequence of pairs. Each pair contributes two parts. The first part must equal the last part, the second part must equal the second to last part, etc.\n\nSo, for example, in the first sample, the split is ab | cd | cd | ab. This can be viewed as two pairs: (ab, ab) and (cd, cd), but arranged in order. So the split is built by choosing pairs of equal parts, and the order of these pairs must form a palindrome.\n\nWait, but the entire split is built by pairs in such a way that each pair is placed symmetrically. So the split is determined by a series of split points that divide the string into pairs.\n\nAnother Idea: The split can be represented by a list of split points in the first half, and the second half must mirror those splits. For example, if the split points are at positions k1, k2, ..., km in the first half, then the split points in the second half must be symmetric. But how to model this.\n\nAlternatively, the split points must be such that the first split is at position k, and the last split is at (n -k), where n is the length of the string. Then, the inner part between k and (n -k) must be split in the same way.\n\nThis recursive splitting suggests that the number of ways is the product of the number of possible split points at each step. For example, if the first step has m possible split points, each leading to a different inner substring, which in turn can have their own split points, the total number of ways is the sum of the products of the possible splits at each level.\n\nBut how to compute this efficiently.\n\nWait, here\'s another approach inspired by the Z-algorithm or prefix function. For each possible split into p and p, we can check if the prefix p equals the corresponding suffix. Then, for each possible p, the number of ways is the number of ways for the inner substring multiplied by 1 (the split into p and p).\n\nBut again, the issue is the time complexity.\n\nLet\'s think of the following steps:\n\n1. Precompute for each possible prefix length l, whether the first l characters equal the last l characters.\n\n2. For each such l, recursively compute the number of ways for the inner substring (from l to n-l).\n\nBut even this is O(n^2), which is not feasible.\n\nAlternative Idea: The problem requires that the entire split is built by layers of pairs. Each layer is a split into a prefix and suffix of equal length. So the total number of ways is the product of the number of possible splits at each layer.\n\nFor example, the first layer can have k possible split lengths (each l such that the first l and last l characters are equal). Then, for each such l, the inner substring (from l to n-l) must be split similarly, and the number of ways for the inner is the product of the split possibilities at each layer.\n\nBut how do we model this?\n\nWait, here\'s an example. Let\'s say the string is split into layers as follows:\n\nLayer 1: l1, so the first l1 and last l1 characters form a pair.\n\nLayer 2: l2, so the next l2 and previous l2 characters (in the inner substring) form a pair.\n\n...\n\nThis process continues until the inner substring is empty.\n\nEach layer\'s split is determined by the possible l_i values, and the total number of ways is the product of the number of choices at each layer.\n\nFor example, if a string can be split in layer 1 into l1, and layer 2 into l2, then the total number of ways is the number of possible l1 choices multiplied by the number of possible l2 choices for each l1.\n\nBut how to compute this efficiently.\n\nHmm. Let\'s model this with a dynamic programming array, where dp[i] is the number of ways to split the substring starting at position i and ending at position n - i. Here, i is the current start of the substring, and n is the original string length. The substring s[i..n-i) must be split into pairs as per the problem\'s condition.\n\nThen, the initial state is dp[0] = 1, since the entire string must be split.\n\nFor each i, we can look at all possible l such that the substring s[i..i+l) equals s[n-i-l..n-i). For each such l, the number of ways would be multiplied by the dp[i+l].\n\nWait, but how to compute this.\n\nWait, here\'s an example. Let\'s say the entire string is s[0..n). For each possible l where s[0..l) == s[n-l..n), then the inner substring is s[l..n-l). The number of ways to split the entire string would be the sum of the number of ways to split the inner substring for each possible l.\n\nBut this is a recursive approach. So dp[i] represents the number of ways to split the substring s[i..n-i). Then:\n\ndp[i] = sum over l of (dp[i + l] if s[i..i+l) == s[n-i - l..n-i) )\n\nThe base case is dp[k] = 1 when i >= k such that the substring is empty (i >= n-i).\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n- Precompute for all possible i and l, whether the prefix and suffix of length l starting at i are equal.\n\n- Then, compute dp[i] as the sum of dp[i + l] for all valid l.\n\nBut how to compute this.\n\nFor each i, we need to find all possible l such that:\n\n- i + l <= (n-i) - l → i + l <= (n -i) → 2i + l <= n → l <= (n - 2i)/2.\n\nWait, the substring s[i..i+l) is the prefix, and the suffix is s[(n-i - l)..(n-i)), which has length l. So the inner substring is s[i+l..n-i - l). So the inner substring\'s length is (n -i - l) - (i + l) ) = n - 2i - 2l. Which must be >=0. So 2i + 2l <=n → i + l <= n/2.\n\nSo for each i, the maximum possible l is (n/2 -i) .\n\nFor example, i=0, l can be from 1 to n/2.\n\nBut for each i, we need to find all l such that the prefix of length l starting at i equals the suffix of length l ending at n-i.\n\nNow, the problem is to compute this efficiently.\n\nTo check if s[i..i+l) equals s[n-i-l..n-i), for all possible l and i, we can precompute a hash for each prefix and suffix.\n\nUsing a rolling hash (like Rabin-Karp) with prefix and suffix hashes would allow us to compare any substring in O(1) time.\n\nSo the plan is:\n\n1. Precompute prefix hashes and suffix hashes for the entire string.\n\n2. For each i, iterate over possible l values (from 1 to (n/2 -i)) and check if the prefix and suffix of length l starting at i are equal.\n\n3. Use dynamic programming to compute dp[i], which is the sum of dp[i + l] for all valid l. The base case is when i >= n/2 (the substring is empty), dp[i] = 1.\n\nBut for n=1e6, this approach would be O(n^2), which is impossible.\n\nSo this approach is not feasible.\n\nAlternative Idea: For each i, find the maximum possible l such that the prefix and suffix are equal, and then see if there\'s a way to split it into smaller l\'s.\n\nBut again, not sure.\n\nWait, but maybe for each position i, the maximum possible l is the largest possible, and the number of ways for dp[i] is the sum of all possible splits. But this still requires O(n) time per i.\n\nAlternative Idea: We can model the problem as a chain of splits. For each possible split into l, the next split is in the inner substring. So the total number of ways is the product of the number of possible l\'s at each step.\n\nBut how to model this with memoization.\n\nAlternatively, think of the problem as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where each node represents a current i (the start position), and edges represent possible l choices. The number of paths from node i to node i + l is the number of ways to split the current substring. The total number of ways is the number of paths from 0 to a node i >=n/2.\n\nBut again, with n=1e6, this approach is not feasible unless we find a way to compute it in O(n) time.\n\nAnother Idea: The problem can be reduced to finding all possible ways to split the string into pairs of layers. Each layer contributes a split into a prefix and suffix of equal length. The total ways is the product of the possible choices at each layer.\n\nBut how to find the product.\n\nWait, let\'s think of the possible layers:\n\nThe first layer is the outermost pair. The next layer is the next pair inside, and so on.\n\nEach layer\'s split must be such that the prefix and suffix are equal. The split can be done at any possible length, but the layers are nested.\n\nThe total number of ways is the product of the number of possible choices at each layer.\n\nBut how to compute this.\n\nAnother Idea: The number of ways is the product of the number of valid split lengths at each possible layer until the string is exhausted.\n\nFor example, the first split has m options, each of which leads to an inner substring that can be split in m\' ways, etc.\n\nBut how to compute this.\n\nBut even this approach would require O(n) layers in the worst case (each split is length 1), and for each layer, compute the possible split options. But with n=1e6, this is O(n^2) again.\n\nAlternative Idea: Precompute for each possible i the number of possible l\'s where the prefix and suffix are equal. Then, the total number of ways is the product of these counts for all possible i\'s in the layers.\n\nBut this seems incorrect, as the choices are not independent.\n\nFor example, choosing l=2 in the first layer may lead to a different set of choices in the next layer than choosing l=1.\n\nSo this approach is not feasible.\n\nAnother Idea: Let\'s think of the problem in terms of the longest possible split at each step. For example, we can always choose the largest possible l at each layer. The total number of ways would then be the product of the number of choices at each layer.\n\nBut this is not correct, because there may be multiple choices at each layer, and the total ways is the sum of all possible paths.\n\nBut perhaps there\'s a way to model this using the multiplicative properties of the choices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is simply the product of the number of possible split lengths at each layer.\n\nBut this seems unlikely.\n\nWait, let\'s take the first sample input: \'abcdcdab\' (n=8).\n\nThe possible split lengths for the first layer are l=2 (ab and ab). The inner substring is \'cdcd\' (n=4). For this substring, the possible split lengths are l=2 (cd and cd). The inner is empty. So the total ways are 1 (first layer choice) multiplied by 1 (second layer choice) = 1.\n\nWhich matches the sample.\n\nAnother sample input: \'abbababababbab\' (n=14). The output is 3. Let\'s see:\n\nThe possible ways are three different combinations of split choices.\n\nFor example:\n\nWay 1: Split into \'ab\' and \'ab\' at layer 1. Then the inner string is \'b ababababab b\' (length 10). Then split into \'b\' and \'b\' at layer 2 (inner length 8). Then split into \'abab\' and \'abab\' (layer 3, inner length 0). So this contributes 1 way.\n\nBut there might be other combinations of split choices. For example, in layer 1, split into \'ab\', layer 2 split into \'b\', layer 3 split into \'abab\', and so on. But how the product is 3.\n\nSo perhaps the total number of ways is the product of the number of choices at each layer. But in this case, how is the product 3?\n\nAlternatively, the number of ways is the sum over all possible split paths. So for each possible path of split layers, the number of ways is the product of the number of choices at each layer.\n\nBut this seems possible.\n\nBut how to compute this efficiently.\n\nHmm. Let\'s think of the problem as a tree of possible split choices. Each node represents a current split state (current i), and edges are the possible l\'s. The number of ways is the sum of the number of leaves in this tree.\n\nBut for large n, this tree can be very deep, and the number of nodes can be O(n^2), which is not feasible.\n\nAlternative Idea: Let\'s use memoization with dynamic programming. But to compute dp[i], the number of ways to split the substring starting at i and ending at n-i, we can compute it by checking all possible l\'s and adding the dp[i+l] values.\n\nBut this requires O(n^2) time, which is not feasible.\n\nBut perhaps the number of valid l\'s for each i is small, making this approach manageable.\n\nBut for a worst-case scenario, such as a string of all \'a\'s, the number of valid l\'s for each i is O(n), leading to O(n^2) time.\n\nThus, this approach is not feasible.\n\nAlternative Idea: For each i, compute the minimal possible l where the prefix and suffix are equal, and then find that the number of ways is 1. But this is not correct.\n\nAlternative Idea: Observe that for the dynamic programming approach, the transitions can be optimized using hashing. For example, for each possible i, we can find the maximum possible l for which the prefix and suffix are equal, and then use that. But this doesn\'t account for all possible l\'s.\n\nBut perhaps the problem has a structure where the maximum possible l for each i is the only possible way to split, leading to a unique path and O(n) time.\n\nBut this is not the case. For example, the second sample input allows three different ways, which suggests that multiple l\'s are possible at various layers.\n\nThus, this approach would not work.\n\nAlternative Idea: Use the fact that for each i, the possible l\'s are divisors of the length of the current substring. For example, for the current substring length L, l must be a divisor of L/2. But I\'m not sure.\n\nAlternatively, think that each possible split l must be such that the current substring has length 2l, and the first l characters equal the last l characters.\n\nWait, no. The current substring has length (n-2i), and each possible l must be such that 2l <= (n-2i). So l can be from 1 to (n-2i)/2.\n\nBut how to find all l\'s for which the prefix and suffix are equal.\n\nThis seems like a problem that can be solved using the Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm\'s failure function or the Z-array.\n\nAlternatively, for each possible i, compute the longest prefix which is also a suffix of the substring s[i..n-i), and then find all possible l\'s that are divisors of that length.\n\nBut this is not clear.\n\nAnother Idea: For each i, compute the longest possible l where s[i..i+l) equals s[n-i-l..n-i). Let this maximum l be l_max. Then, any l that is a divisor of l_max and the substring can be split into a repetition of the prefix of length l.\n\nBut I\'m not sure.\n\nAlternatively, for each i, the possible l\'s are all possible lengths where s[i..i+l) equals s[n-i-l..n-i). To find these l\'s efficiently, perhaps we can use the prefix function for the concatenated string.\n\nFor example, for each i, consider the substring s[i..n-i). We can concatenate this substring with a separator and its reverse, then compute the prefix function. The maximum l for which the prefix and suffix are equal is given by the prefix function of the concatenated string. But this approach may not capture all possible l\'s.\n\nAlternatively, for each i, compute the longest possible l_max and then all possible l\'s are the divisors of l_max. But this is not necessarily true.\n\nThis line of thinking is not leading me to an efficient solution.\n\nGiven the time constraints, perhaps I need to look for an O(n) approach with some pre-processing.\n\nLet\'s think again about the problem. The number of ways is the sum over all possible split l\'s at each layer. So, for the first layer, possible l\'s are l1, for each l1, the inner substring is processed similarly.\n\nBut this is a recursive problem, and perhaps we can model it using a dynamic programming array where we track the number of ways for each possible i.\n\nBut with n=1e6, we need an O(n) solution.\n\nAnother Idea: The number of ways is the product of the number of possible splits at each possible layer. For example, the first layer can have x ways, the second layer y ways, etc. The total is x * y * ... * z.\n\nBut how to find the layers.\n\nAlternatively, the answer is the number of possible ways to split the string into layers where each layer is a pair of equal substrings. The layers are nested, and each layer\'s split must be into a prefix and suffix of equal length.\n\nSo the process is:\n\n- Split the entire string into l1 and l1.\n\n- Split the inner string into l2 and l2.\n\n- Continue until the inner string is empty.\n\nThe total number of ways is the product of the number of possible choices for each li at each layer.\n\nBut how to compute this product.\n\nEach layer\'s possible choices are the number of possible l\'s that can split the current substring into a pair.\n\nFor example, if the current substring is s[i..j), then the number of possible l\'s is the number of l\'s such that s[i..i+l) == s[j-l..j).\n\nThe product of the number of choices for each layer gives the total number of ways.\n\nBut this is only true if the choices are made in sequence. However, the layers can be split in multiple ways, leading to different layers.\n\nBut the problem is that for a given string, there can be multiple possible paths of layers. For example, the first layer can be split into l1=2, leading to a certain inner substring, or l1=3, leading to a different inner substring. Each of these contributes to the total number of ways.\n\nThus, the total number of ways is the sum of the products of choices along all possible paths of layers.\n\nBut this seems impossible to compute for large n.\n\nBut perhaps there\'s a way to model this using a dynamic programming array where dp[i] is the number of ways to split the substring starting at i and ending at n-i.\n\nThen, dp[i] is the sum of dp[i+l] for all valid l\'s.\n\nThe base case is dp[i] = 1 when i >= n/2.\n\nBut how to compute this efficiently.\n\nWith this approach, the answer is dp[0].\n\nBut for each i, how to compute the sum over all valid l\'s of dp[i + l].\n\nThe problem is that for each i, we need to find all possible l\'s where s[i..i+l) equals s[n-i - l..n-i), and sum dp[i + l] for each such l.\n\nBut for n=1e6, this is O(n^2) time in the worst case.\n\nBut perhaps, with certain optimizations, we can compute this in O(n) time.\n\nLet\'s think of how to find all valid l\'s for each i.\n\nFor a given i, the valid l\'s are the l\'s such that the prefix of length l equals the suffix of length l of the substring s[i..n-i).\n\nTo compute these l\'s efficiently, we can use a rolling hash.\n\nPrecompute the prefix and suffix hashes for the string. Then, for each i, iterate l from 1 to (n-2i)/2 and check if the hash of s[i..i+l) equals the hash of s[n-i - l..n-i).\n\nBut for each i, this is O((n-2i)/2) time, which totals O(n^2) time.\n\nThis is not feasible.\n\nAlternative Idea: The number of valid l\'s for each i is 1. Then, the total time is O(n).\n\nBut this is not necessarily true.\n\nAnother Idea: For each i, compute the maximum possible l_max and then only consider l_max. But this would miss smaller valid l\'s.\n\nBut perhaps the answer is simply the number of times you can split the string into the maximum possible l at each step. For example, if the maximum possible l is chosen each time, then the number of ways is 1. But this is only correct if there are no other valid splits.\n\nBut the sample input 2 has three ways, which suggests that there are multiple valid splits.\n\nThus, this approach is not correct.\n\nAlternative Idea: The problem is similar to counting the number of distinct decompositions into a sequence of even-length palindromic pairs, but with even parts.\n\nBut I\'m not sure.\n\nAnother Idea: The problem can be modeled using memoization with the help of the Z-array.\n\nFor each i, compute the maximum l_max where the prefix and suffix are equal. The number of ways for i is the sum of the ways for i + l, where l is any divisor of l_max or a length that can form a valid split.\n\nBut I\'m not sure.\n\nAlternatively, for each i, the valid l\'s are all possible lengths that are borders of the substring s[i..n-i). A border is a prefix which is also a suffix. The set of borders for a string can be found using the KMP failure function.\n\nFor example, for the substring s[i..n-i), compute all borders, and those are the possible l\'s. The sum of the dp[i + l] for each such l.\n\nThe KMP failure function for the substring can be computed in O(len) time, where len is the length of the substring.\n\nBut for each i, the substring len is (n-2i). So for n=1e6, this is O(n^2) time.\n\nThus, this approach is not feasible.\n\nGiven the time constraints, I think the correct approach is to use dynamic programming with hashing, but optimized with precomputed prefix and suffix hashes. But given the time constraints of the problem, perhaps it\'s possible to precompute the hash for all possible prefixes and suffixes, and then for each i, iterate l from 1 to possible maximum, check hash equality, and sum the dp[i+l].\n\nBut for n=1e6, this would be O(n^2), which is not feasible.\n\nBut wait, what\'s the maximum possible number of valid l\'s for each i?\n\nFor example, if the string is all \'a\'s, then for any i, the valid l\'s are 1, 2, ..., floor((n-2i)/2). So for i=0, l can be up to 5e5 for n=1e6. This would be O(n) time per i, leading to O(n^2) time, which is not feasible.\n\nThus, this approach is not possible.\n\nTherefore, I must think of a different approach.\n\nAlternative Idea: The only way to have a valid split is if the entire string can be split into pairs of equal parts. The number of ways is the number of possible ways to split the string into such pairs.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nAnother Idea: The problem can be viewed as building a palindrome-like structure where each layer is a pair of equal parts. The number of ways is the product of the number of choices for each layer.\n\nThis suggests that the answer is 2^{k-1} where k is the number of possible splits. But this is probably incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, the answer is the product of the number of possible splits at each layer.\n\nBut how to find the layers.\n\nWait, perhaps the layers are determined by the possible split points where the first split can be made, and then the inner substring is processed recursively.\n\nFor example, the total number of ways for the entire string is the sum of the number of ways for each possible first split.\n\nBut this is the same as the dynamic programming approach, which is O(n^2) time.\n\nBut given the time constraints, perhaps there\'s an observation that can reduce the complexity.\n\nObservation: For a split to be valid, each pair of parts must be equal. So the entire string must be a concatenation of pairs of equal parts. For example, the split is p1, p2, p2, p1. So the string is p1 p2 p2 p1. But this is the same as p1 concatenated with p2 concatenated with their mirror images.\n\nBut how does this help?\n\nAlternative Idea: The split must form a palindromic sequence of parts. So the entire split must be a palindrome of parts. Thus, the entire split is determined by the first m parts, where m is k/2. The remaining m parts are the reverse of the first m parts.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to finding the number of ways to split the first half of the string into parts and then mirror them in the second half.\n\nBut the entire string\'s length must be even, which it is.\n\nSo the idea is to split the first half of the string into parts, and then mirror those parts in the second half. For this to be possible, the second half of the string must exactly mirror the first half\'s split.\n\nFor example, for the first sample input, the first half is \'abcd\' and the second half is \'cdab\'. The split is ab | cd in the first half, and cd | ab in the second half. Thus, the split of the entire string is ab | cd | cd | ab.\n\nBut this approach requires that the second half is the mirror of the first half\'s split.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to splitting the first half into parts such that the concatenation of those parts is equal to the first half, and the concatenation of their reverses is equal to the second half.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nLet me denote the first half as S, and the second half as T. The split must be such that the parts of the first half form a sequence p1, p2, ..., pm, and the second half must be the sequence pm, pm-1, ..., p1.\n\nThus, the concatenation of the first half\'s parts is S = p1p2...pm, and the second half\'s parts must be pm pm-1 ... p1. Thus, T must equal pm pm-1 ... p1.\n\nSo the problem is to find the number of ways to split S into parts such that the concatenation of those parts reversed equals T.\n\nBut the entire string is S followed by T. We need to split the entire string into pairs of parts where the parts in the second half are the reverse of the first half\'s parts.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to finding the number of ways to split S into parts p1, p2, ..., pm such that the concatenation of pm, pm-1, ..., p1 is equal to T.\n\nThis is a different problem, but perhaps easier to model.\n\nFor example, for the first sample input, S is \'abcd\', T is \'cdab\'. The split is p1=\'ab\', p2=\'cd\'. Then, the concatenation of p2 and p1 is \'cdab\', which equals T.\n\nSo the problem now is to find the number of ways to split S into parts, then check if the reversed concatenation of those parts equals T.\n\nBut how to model this efficiently.\n\nThis seems like a dynamic programming problem where for each position i in S, we track the possible splits and check if the remaining part of S can be split such that their reversed concatenation matches T.\n\nBut given the size of S (up to 5e5 characters for n=1e6), this approach is still O(n^2) and not feasible.\n\nBut perhaps this can be optimized using some hashing or string matching algorithm.\n\nAnother Idea: Precompute the hash of T, and for each possible split point in S, compute the corresponding reversed concatenation\'s hash and check if it matches T\'s hash.\n\nBut this is not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, for a split of S into p1, p2, ..., pm, the corresponding T must be pmpm-1...p1. So the entire T is the concatenation of the reversed parts.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nWait, perhaps the split of S into parts is such that the parts form a decomposition that, when reversed, must match T.\n\nThus, the problem is to find the number of ways to split S into parts such that their reversed concatenation equals T.\n\nThis is a classic problem that can be modeled with dynamic programming.\n\nLet\'s define dp[i] as the number of ways to split the first i characters of S into parts such that the reversed concatenation of those parts equals the first i characters of T.\n\nWait, no. Because the entire S must be split into parts, and their reversed concatenation must equal T.\n\nSo for S of length m and T of length m, we need to split S into p1, p2, ..., pk such that p1 || p2 || ... || pk = S, and pk || pk-1 || ... || p1 = T.\n\nThe number of ways to split S into such parts is the answer to the problem.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to counting the number of ways to split S into a sequence of parts whose reversed concatenation equals T.\n\nBut how to compute this.\n\nThis is a classic dynamic programming problem. Let\'s think of it as follows:\n\nWe need to split S into parts such that when reversed, they form T. So for each possible split of S into parts, we check if their reversed order equals T.\n\nBut for large m (up to 5e5), this approach is O(m^2), which is not feasible.\n\nBut perhaps we can model it using dynamic programming and hash checks.\n\nLet\'s define dp[i] as the number of ways to split the first i characters of S into parts such that the reversed concatenation of those parts equals the first i characters of T.\n\nThe base case is dp[0] = 1.\n\nFor each i, we can try all possible j < i such that the substring S[j..i) is equal to the substring T[m-i..m-j). Then, dp[i] += dp[j].\n\nHere, m is the length of S (and T).\n\nThis way, for each i, we look for possible j\'s such that the substring S[j..i) is the next part in the reversed concatenation. Because when reversed, the parts are added in reverse order, the next part in the reversed concatenation must be added to the beginning of T. So the substring T\'s first (i) characters must be the concatenation of the reversed parts up to i.\n\nFor example, suppose we have parts p1, p2. The reversed concatenation is p2 p1. So the first part in the reversed concatenation is p2, then p1. So in T, the first len(p2) characters must be p2, followed by len(p1) characters of p1.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nThe dynamic programming approach would be to process each position i in S (from 1 to m), and for each possible j < i, check if S[j..i) equals T\'s substring T[m-i .. m-j). Then, add dp[j] to dp[i].\n\nBut for m=5e5, this is O(m^2), which is not feasible.\n\nThus, this approach is also not feasible.\n\nBut perhaps there\'s a way to compute this using the KMP algorithm or the hashing trick.\n\nFor example, we can precompute the hash of every possible substring of S and T, and then for each i, compute the maximum possible length l such that the substring S[i-l..i) equals T\'s substring T[m-i..m-i + l).\n\nWait, that\'s an interesting Idea. Let\'s think about it.\n\nThe reversed concatenation of the parts must be equal to T. So, the first part in the reversed concatenation is the last part of the original split. For example, if the split is p1, p2, then the reversed concatenation is p2 p1, which must equal T. So, the first part of T is p2, the next part is p1.\n\nSo, when building the dynamic programming solution, for each i (current position in S), the parts are added such that their reversed order forms T. So, for each position i in S, the next part added to the split is a substring S[j..i) in S, which must match the substring T[prev..prev + len) in T, where prev is the current length of T processed.\n\nThus, the relationship is that the next part added to the split (in reversed order) must match the corresponding part in T.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nAnother Idea: We can construct a new string by concatenating S and T, and precompute their hash values. Then, for each possible i in S, check for the longest l such that S[i-l..i) equals T\'s substring T[ (m -i).. (m -i) + l ). This l is the maximum possible part length that can be added at this step.\n\nBut even this requires O(m) time per i, leading to O(m^2) time.\n\nBut perhaps we can precompute all possible l\'s for all i using a suffix automaton or other advanced data structure.\n\nBut this is beyond my current knowledge.\n\nAlternative Idea: The problem can be reduced to finding the number of decompositions of S into parts whose concatenation is such that their reversed order forms T. This is equivalent to the concatenation of the parts in S being S, and the concatenation of the reversed parts in reverse order being T.\n\nSo, for example, if the parts are p1, p2, ..., pk, then T must be pk || pk-1 || ... || p1.\n\nThis is similar to the problem of finding all possible ways to split S into parts such that the reversed concatenation is T. This is a known problem, but I\'m not aware of an efficient solution for large m.\n\nGiven the time constraints and the need to find an efficient solution, I\'m stuck. Perhaps there\'s a pattern or a mathematical approach that I\'m missing.\n\nAlternative Idea: The problem requires that the entire string is partitioned into pairs of substrings that are mirrored. So the number of ways is equal to the number of ways to split the first half of the string into parts such that the reversed concatenation of these parts equals the second half of the string.\n\nThus, the answer is the number of ways to split the first half into parts where their reversed concatenation equals the second half.\n\nThis is exactly the problem described earlier. So, the problem reduces to finding the number of ways to split the first half S into parts such that their reversed concatenation equals T (the second half).\n\nTo solve this efficiently, we can model it using dynamic programming with hashing.\n\nLet\'s proceed with this approach.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Split the input string into two halves: S and T.\n\n   S is the first half, T is the second half.\n\n   For example, input \'abcdcdab\' is split into S = \'abcd\' and T = \'cdab\'.\n\n2. Compute the number of ways to split S into parts such that the concatenation of the reversed parts equals T.\n\nThis is the answer.\n\nNow, how to compute this.\n\nWe can model this with dynamic programming. Let dp[i] be the number of ways to split the first i characters of S into parts such that their reversed concatenation equals the first i characters of T.\n\nThe base case is dp[0] = 1.\n\nFor each position i in S, we look for all possible j < i such that the substring S[j..i) equals the substring T[i - j..i) of T (assuming T is the same length as S).\n\nWait, no. Let me think: the reversed concatenation of the parts up to i must equal the first i characters of T.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a split of S into parts p1, p2. Their reversed concatenation is p2 p1. The total length is len(p2) + len(p1) = i. This must equal the first i characters of T.\n\nSo, for each i, we need to find all j < i such that the substring S[j..i) equals the substring T[0..i - j) (since the reversed concatenation starts from the first part).\n\nWait, no. The reversed concatenation is the parts in reverse order. So if we are adding a new part of length l = i-j, this part will be added to the beginning of the reversed concatenation. So the first l characters of T must be this new part, and the remaining i-l characters must be formed by the existing parts.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a current state where we have processed j characters of S, which correspond to the reversed concatenation being some string. When we add a new part of length l = i-j (S[j..i)), this part becomes the first part in the reversed concatenation. Thus, the first l characters of T must be S[j..i), and the next (j) characters of T must be the previous reversed concatenation.\n\nBut this is getting complicated. Let\'s re-express it.\n\nAt each step i in S, we are processing the first i characters of S. The reversed concatenation of the parts in S up to i must be the first i characters of T.\n\nSo, when we split S into parts p1, p2, ..., pk, their reversed concatenation is pk ... p2 p1, which must equal T\'s first i characters.\n\nTo compute this, for each position i, we can look for all possible j < i such that the substring S[j..i) equals the substring T[i - (i-j) .. i) = T[j..i).\n\nWait, no. The reversed concatenation up to i characters in S is the concatenation of the parts in reverse order. So the new part added from j to i is the last part in the original split, but the first part in the reversed concatenation.\n\nSo, for example, if we are adding a part of length l = i-j (S[j..i)), then in the reversed concatenation, this part is added to the beginning. So, the first l characters of T must be S[j..i), and the remaining j characters must match the reversed concatenation of the parts processed up to j.\n\nThus, for each i, to compute dp[i], we